{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 8, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Summary of the Wikipedia page \"Raymond III Count of Tripoli\"**\n\nRaymond III (c. 1140 – 1187) was Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III became count at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated in 1152. Due to his young age he was placed under the regency of his mother Hodierna and his uncle King Baldwin III of Jerusalem.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states. In 1164 he was captured by Nur ad-Din at the Battle of Harim and was held captive for nearly ten years. During his captivity his mother Hodierna and then his cousin Bohemond III of Antioch ruled Tripoli. Raymond was released in 1174 after the death of Nur ad-Din. Upon his return he resumed control of Tripoli and continued to be a key figure in the region.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\nRaymond III served as regent for King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem from 1174 to 1177 and again from 1185 to 1186. As regent he worked to maintain the fragile balance of power between the Crusader states and the Muslim powers. He supported the succession of Baldwin V and later attempted to secure the throne for Guy of Lusignan. Raymond was also involved in the conflicts between the Crusader states and the Byzantine Empire.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 shortly before the Battle of Hattin. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli. He was succeeded by his distant cousin Raymond IV of Tripoli who was also known as Raymond of Toulouse. Raymond III’s legacy is marked by his efforts to maintain stability and unity among the Crusader states during a period of increasing Muslim power and internal Christian divisions.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.17142000794410706}, {"token": "Summary", "l